  "notes": "Italo-house/Eurodance with a euphoric female lead. The entry names one instrument as the identity — \"piano stabs are the lead instrument, with a saw or organ pad behind\" — and one voicing to go with it, \"stabbed triads in the right hand, root octaves in the left\", so the chords are close-voiced, triad-heavy at 0.70, and the melody carries a piano timbre hint rather than a saw one. The kit is the entry's own: kick on all four, layered clap on 2&4, offbeat open hats, a 16th shaker, and \"Italo-house tom fills into the chorus\", which is why the perc lanes are shaker plus two tom lanes rather than the parent's snap/shaker/tambourine. The bass is the documented Italo signature — \"octave-jumping syncopated synth bass moving against the kick\" — offbeat 8ths with an octave passing tone. The topline is \"single-female-voice, wide-interval and sustained, with the title hook as a two-bar cell\", hence a legato articulation, a wide leap share and 2-bar phrasing. ⚠ The research marks the harmonic and voicing reading `[LOW-CONF]`, calling it Italo-house/Eurodance convention rather than act documentation, and that mark is repeated here. ⚠ All velocities, densities and probabilities above are INFERRED from those adjectives.",
